A||N||14||aubicon||legal question||||1||
Z||000000||aubicon||06-17-2001||08:27 PM||||I am completly rebuilding my fiero so i stripped the thing all the way to frame and now i am refinishing the frame. i know that i must not touch the vin plates because if those are even scrached the vehical will have to go through an investigation to see if it is stolen. but i have another problem there is a sticker on the inside of the front fender on the drivers side, the sticker is the survice part identification, it says do not remove. is this just a dealer recomondation for survice conveniance, or is there some legal reason that it is there.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Seanh||06-17-2001||08:30 PM||||You can remove it, but keep it in tact and save it for future reference. It's perfectly legal to remove it, its only for parts identification, should you ever need it.||||reg||1
Z||000002||aubicon||06-17-2001||08:50 PM||||thanks for the help, this is the best site i have ever found.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Songman||06-17-2001||08:58 PM||||There is supposed to be a sticker with the VIN number on every body panel of the car. This is how you can tell if anything has been replaced on the car... But I don't know of any law that says you can't remove them or paint over them. But that doesn't mean there isn't such a law. I will remove them whenever I repaint any of my cars...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Toddster||06-17-2001||09:04 PM||||removal is difficult so before you do it, take a picture (preferably polaroid or digital so you can check clarity right away). Then take the picture, scan it in and using a good package like Photoshop8 you can reproduce it exactly! looks good for those resto projects.||||reg||1
Z||000005||TRiAD||06-17-2001||09:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Songman:<BR><B>There is supposed to be a sticker with the VIN number on every body panel of the car. This is how you can tell if anything has been replaced on the car... But I don't know of any law that says you can't remove them or paint over them. But that doesn't mean there isn't such a law. I will remove them whenever I repaint any of my cars...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Is this true of Fieros?<BR>There are <B>very few</B> cars in the world that carry the VIN (or part of it) on all panels...Lamborghini Miura comes to mind. <P>Most vehicles carry it on the Vin plate, the dr. door and possibly in the trunk/hatch, and some/most newer vehicles have stickers in all openings (trunk, doors, hood, etc.). <P>Anyway, nice question!<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||5
Z||000006||rockcrawl||06-17-2001||09:28 PM||||GM only started doing this in '87, and only on the body panels. Many times they come off very easily.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||06-17-2001||09:35 PM||||As I recall, it became federal law that the VIN be on every body panel beginning with '87 model year vehicles.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Necromancer||06-17-2001||10:21 PM||||My '87 GT is plastered with those stickers everywhere. they are on the top edge of the fenders (where the bolts go), under the license plates, etc. I even found one under the console where the shifter innards are!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||06-17-2001||11:00 PM||||The VIN stickers that started in 87 are federally mandated. It is also a crime to remove them. They fall off? I'm not sure what you are exposing them too. I don't think I want that kind of stuff on/near me. They are made to not fall off even under rather severe conditions. Nearly all of mine are in like new condition.<P>What makes things complicated is that because fiero crosses the line of when those are required, it is entirely common for the labels to be missing due to older parts being installed. (My doors with power windows fit this catagory.)<P>If you buy anything with those sticker on it, make sure you get good reciepts from the scrap yard that include the VIN. Failing to do so can cause major headaches. Police know to look for those stickers. If they find ones that don't match your VIN you could have some fast talking to do. (Keep in mind, recent US Supreme Court rullings make it really easy for cops to search a car. They usually look for drugs but don't take chances.)<P>As for the RPO/Service label on the left fender. Don't demove it unless you absolutly have to. It is the only way for you to prove the car's original equipment at time of resale. If you do take it off, do as stated above and make sure you have a readable photo first. You may need data off that label later.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Vader||06-17-2001||11:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>... They are made to not fall off even under rather severe conditions....<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>And the rear upper strut braces are not designed to rot out and cause the car to buckle, but guess what happens after 15 years of midwestern winter salt exposure.<P>I've replaced both strut braces (had to remove the upper body half to do it right) AND my RPO label.  It is important to have a good, accurate RPO label, but I don't think there is a federal mandate.  I'm fortunate in that making self-adhesive vinyl labels is easy.  I've got fresh copies of the RPO, VECI, and several of the alpha code stickers from various parts and assemblies that were damaged or missing over the years.<P> <IMG SRC="http://homestead.juno.com/jlstrey/files/VECILabel.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://homestead.juno.com/jlstrey/files/84RPO.jpg"> <P>It was a lotta fun finding the correct fonts, but after that...<P>Oh, yeah, bar codes are easy, too.<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Let the bodies hit the floor!</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000011||Songman||06-17-2001||11:36 PM||||My cars, at this point, have all of the VIN stickers. My 88 T-top has a solid white sticker that used to have RPOs on it. I don't know why it is that way, but it is. I would love to find a way to reproduce it. Paul that does the T-top database gave me a list of RPOs for my car if I could get the sticker made.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Tigger||06-18-2001||12:48 AM||||Hey, I was seeing those stickers on the fenders and whatnot of Fieros at a show this spring.  I was scratching my head everytime I saw one that day saying, "What the heck, I don't remember those stickers on my '84 Fiero?"  Now I think I know why.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Yes, keep the RPO code if all possible.||||reg||3
Z||000013||cadaver||06-18-2001||01:30 AM||||First off if you are replacing a fender and are worried about the code on that sticker not matching then remnove it. You dont need to keep all these records and such.<P>As for the blank RPO sticker. I noticed on the early cars they are covered with a piece of clear mylar tape. On the later ones they are not. So the codes just weather off and leave you a blank one.<P>Probably a way for GM to save money not buying tape.<P>As for duplicating that sticker with no record or it good luck. I have a most complete list of the codes and I couldnt come up with all the ones from my car.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Songman||06-18-2001||02:38 AM||||That's kinda what I figured, Tom...<P>Besides, I'm not too worried about it. Not much is going to be as it was when I am finished with this particular car anyway...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
